WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.642
Hello, my name is Zisl Slepovitch

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.759
and I'm musician-in-residence at Yale

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.890
University's Fortunoff Video Archive

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.549
for Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.630
This video series is designed as a companion

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.790
to our album Where Is Our Homeland,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.178
which is the first volume in the Archive's

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:51.829
ongoing effort to present songs

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.959
and poems performed by survivors in

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.539
testimonies given to the Fortunoff Archive

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.499
over the last 40 years.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.889
You can learn more about the project on the

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.528
Archive's website, songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.859
Where Is Our Homeland?

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.839
Songs From Testimonies Volume One

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.329
was recorded in 2019

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.508
by the Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble, featuring

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.890
renowned singer Sasha Lurje,

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.099
Craig Judelman on the violin, Joshua

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.349
Camp on the accordion, keyboards and

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.569
guitar, Dmitry Ishenko on the

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.819
bass, and myself on woodwinds.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.909
The songs in this collection differ vastly

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.870
in terms of style, social and historical

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.739
background language and many other

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.199
aspects, but collectively they

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.964
create a multidimensional picture of

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.195
Jewish life before and during World

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.030
War II.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.979
Fortunoff Video Archive testimonies hold

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.300
a wide variety of songs and

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:50.460
poetry.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.180
The prewar pieces alone

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.189
form quite a large group.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.380
They include traditional songs

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:58.796
of many genres, military

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.689
songs, badkhones, the

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.209
wedding jesters who plays and

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.549
recitatives, Zionist songs,

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.250
Bundist songs, communist songs,

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:13.130
really a wide variety of

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:14.779
music and poetry.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:16.839
Jack M., a native of the Polish

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:18.570
town of Szydlowiec,

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:22.130
is remembering the songs that

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.994
he heard and remembered from the prewar

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.370
period.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.229
They're not only important because

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.630
they constitute

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.419
a large part

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.420
of the ethnographic research

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.980
of Polish Jewry, of Polish culture, of

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.100
Jewish culture.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.220
But they also portray

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.500
how the people were really

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.069
formed and shaped

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:50.469
before the war.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:54.060
And they help us

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:56.489
understand who

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.450
those people really were as normal human

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.800
beings, not as just inmates,

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.300
as prisoners at concentration

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.759
camps and slave labor camps,

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.039
but human beings,

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.039
Polish citizens, Jews.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.120
In his testimony, Jack is remembering

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.509
these songs that he

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:22.020
heard as he was serving in the

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:23.180
Polish Army.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:26.979
And those are quite interesting ones.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.070
The two songs that he remembered

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.760
were coming mostly from his commander

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:34.980
in his regiment.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.460
And one is quite openly antisemitic

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.480
and the other one is rather frivolous.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.390
Jack remembered them, he did not

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:46.920
consider them

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.310
really negative,

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.340
but that probably tells us

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.399
about the degree of tolerance

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.109
that the targeted

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.153
group of population, groups, societal

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.100
group develops as a survival

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.300
tool.

00:04:11.764 --> 00:04:13.782
Eh, we were laughing

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.129
at, on us, the Christians.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.159
Antisemitic all around me,

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:22.919
the, they made up a song,

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.080
the took, they took a

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.569
Jew, took them to the Army

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.100
in,

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:32.340
in.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:36.100
